<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 20px;">
    <h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; margin-top: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 20px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

    <div style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 15px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <table style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%;">
            <tbody>
                <tr style="border-bottom: 1px dashed #ccc;">
                    <td style="padding: 8px; border-right: 1px dashed #ccc; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
                    <td style="padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
                </tr>
                <tr style="border-bottom: 1px dashed #ccc;">
                    <td style="padding: 8px; border-right: 1px dashed #ccc;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
                    <td style="padding: 8px; border-right: 1px dashed #ccc;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Nature’s Wonders</td>
                    <td style="padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Slow, Furry Sloth!</td>
                </tr>
                <tr style="border-bottom: 1px dashed #ccc;">
                    <td style="padding: 8px; border-right: 1px dashed #ccc;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
                    <td style="padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed #ccc;" colspan="3">
                        <b>Specific Objective:</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reading: Show comprehension of simple sentences.<br>Reading: Follow written instructions with visual aids.<br>Phonemic Awareness: Recognize all the letters of the alphabet in isolation and the correspondence between letter and sound at the beginning of words.</p>
                        <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 0 0;">Reading: Can show comprehension of simple sentences by identifying key details (e.g., "The sloth is slow.").<br>Can follow written instructions by matching written descriptions with the corresponding pictures (e.g., a description of the rainforest and its picture).<br>Can identify the initial and final phonemes of spoken words related to nature and make the correspondence between letters and their sounds at the beginning of words with visual support (e.g., J /dʒ/ "jungle" supported with a picture).</p>
                    </td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; border-bottom: 2px solid #1a5276; padding-bottom: 5px; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays large flashcards of rainforest animals (sloth, toucan, monkey, parrot, frog, snake). The teacher points to each animal and asks, "What animal is this?" Students respond chorally (e.g., "Sloth!"). The teacher then asks, "Is this a slow sloth?" or "Is the toucan colorful?" focusing on `adjectives + nouns` and `WH-questions` for review from Lesson 1.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a sloth and clearly pronounces "sloth" emphasizing the /s/ sound. The teacher then holds up a picture of a rainforest and pronounces "rainforest" emphasizing the /r/ sound. The teacher models a simple sentence: "This is a slow sloth." pointing to the picture and running a finger under the words, demonstrating how to read.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and concepts.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a sloth) "Is the sloth fast?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a toucan) "Is the toucan colorful?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, illustrated short text (projected or on chart paper) titled "A Day in the Rainforest" with simple sentences and clear pictures. The text uses `adjectives + nouns` (e.g., slow sloth, colorful toucan, leafy vines), `present simple` (e.g., It's the rainforest, sloths live, monkeys swing), and key `vocabulary` (rainforest, sloth, toucan, monkey, river, vines, branch, leaf).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example text: "This is the rainforest. It's green and tall. Look! This is a slow, furry sloth. The sloth lives on a branch. It sleeps a lot. This is a colorful toucan. The toucan flies in the sky. This is a small monkey. The monkey swings on leafy vines near the river. The rainforest is home to many animals. It is wonderful!"</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading and understanding basic information from a short, simple text with visual support.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher reads the text aloud, pointing to words and pictures. Then asks: "What is the story about?" (Expected: "Rainforest animals" or "Sloth"). "What animal is slow?" (Expected: "Sloth").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads simple sentences and students show "True" (thumbs up) or "False" (thumbs down).
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The sloth is fast." (False)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The toucan is colorful." (True)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Monkeys live in the river." (False)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading simple sentences, identifying key details, matching descriptions to pictures, recognizing initial phonemes (`/s/` for sloth, `/r/` for rainforest, `/dʒ/` for jungle). Using `adjectives + nouns`, `present simple` verbs, and `nature vocabulary`.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Read and Match:</b> Students receive a worksheet with three simple sentences on one side and three corresponding pictures (e.g., a slow sloth, a colorful toucan, a monkey swinging) on the other. Students read each sentence (e.g., "The sloth is slow and furry." "The toucan is colorful.") and draw a line to match it with the correct picture. The teacher circulates, provides support, and checks for correct matches.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Read and Circle:</b> Students receive a worksheet with short descriptions and two picture options or two word options. They read the sentence and circle the correct answer.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This animal is green and jumps. It is a (frog / snake)." (Picture of a frog and a snake.)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The sloth is (slow / jumpy)." (Picture of a sloth and a monkey.)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is a tall (river / forest)." (Picture of a river and a forest.)</li>
                            </ul>
                            The teacher guides students through the first example chorally.
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Find the Initial Sound:</b> The teacher presents a few large flashcards (e.g., "Jungle", "Sloth", "Rainforest", "Frog"). Students work in pairs. One student picks a card, says the initial sound (e.g., "/dʒ/"), and the other student points to the corresponding letter on a letter chart or written word on the card. Then they both read the word. The teacher reinforces the connection between letter and sound.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: Our Rainforest Scene</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a worksheet with a basic outline of a rainforest (trees, a river line, a general area for sky/ground). They also receive a set of simple written instructions with visual aids (small picture next to each instruction). Students read each instruction and either draw the item or glue pre-cut pictures (e.g., sloth, toucan, monkey, leafy vine, waterfall) onto their rainforest scene.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Instructions examples:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Draw a slow, furry sloth on a branch. (Picture of sloth)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Glue a colorful toucan flying near the top of a tall tree. (Picture of toucan)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Draw a monkey swinging on leafy vines. (Picture of monkey on vine)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Color the river blue and the leaves green. (Picture of river/leaves)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. Draw a waterfall in the river. (Picture of waterfall)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher models the first instruction: "Read 'Draw a slow, furry sloth on a branch.' Now, I draw my sloth here." Students work individually, focusing on reading comprehension and following instructions accurately. The teacher walks around, observes, and offers help with reading or vocabulary.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Our Rainforest Scene" activity. The teacher checks if students correctly read and followed the written instructions by observing their drawings/placements. A quick informal check on initial sound recognition for a few students.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The completed "Our Rainforest Scene" worksheet.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can show comprehension of simple sentences by identifying key details (e.g., drawing a "slow sloth" not a fast one).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can follow written instructions accurately by matching descriptions with corresponding actions/pictures.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify the initial phoneme of target words (e.g., for "jungle," "sloth," "rainforest") when prompted verbally or visually.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to use target vocabulary (e.g., "sloth," "rainforest," "colorful," "slow") in interaction with the teacher.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with two prompts:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read about a sloth." (Circle: Yes / No / A little)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can follow simple instructions." (Circle: Yes / No / A little)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and Teacher reflection, connection to next lesson.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b> The teacher asks the students:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to read today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult to read today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"The students generally showed good comprehension of simple sentences and followed visual instructions well. The 'Read and Match' activity was effective for reinforcing vocabulary and basic sentence structure. The phonemic awareness activity needed more explicit modeling for some students, perhaps using an alphabet chart with corresponding animal pictures. Some students struggled with differentiating between 'frog' and 'snake' in the 'Read and Circle' task, indicating a need for more visual and auditory reinforcement of those specific words. I should ensure the text is even more visually supported for the next reading task."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great job reading about our slow, furry sloths and the rainforest today! Next time, we will talk more about these amazing animals and practice describing them to our friends!"</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>